{"id":"n-20261027-nl0wnu","category":"health","display_title":"The Special Education Shortage","display_title_he":"מחסור במורי חינוך מיוחד","short_summary":"Public schools across the United States face a persistent shortage of special education teachers as student needs increase and administrative burdens grow.","english_level":"C1","words":480,"reading_time":3,"image":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Medical_Department_-_Restoration_Work_-_Education_and_Rehab_-_Disabled_French_soldiers_turn_their_efforts_toward_lessening_food_shortage_in_France._Wounded_ex-soldier_leading_a_bullock_team_attached_to_plow_assis%28...%29_-_NARA_-_45498375.jpg/960px-thumbnail.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Unknown author Unknown author or not provided · Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Medical_Department_-_Restoration_Work_-_Education_and_Rehab_-_Disabled_French_soldiers_turn_their_efforts_toward_lessening_food_shortage_in_France._Wounded_ex-soldier_leading_a_bullock_team_attached_to_plow_assis(...)_-_NARA_-_45498375.jpg"},"sources":[{"name":"NPR","domain":"npr.org","url":"https://www.npr.org/2026/10/26/nx-s1-6000653/special-education-teacher-shortage","title":"Special ed diaries: Teachers tell NPR about their most joyous, most difficult moments","published_at":"2026-10-26T09:00:00.000Z"}],"published_at":"2026-10-26T09:00:00.000Z","first_published_in_app_at":"2026-10-27T06:34:55.486Z","quality_score":9,"interest_score":8,"verification_status":"verified","content_status":"published","paragraphs":["Across the United States, public schools continue to grapple with a persistent shortage of special education teachers. According to the Learning Policy Institute, a specialized research organization, shortages of these educators now affect 45 out of 50 states. This gap between supply and demand is expanding because the overall population of special education students is climbing steadily, currently exceeding 8.2 million children, or roughly one out of every six public school students. Although educator turnover intensified sharply in the wake of the COVID-19 pandemic, the underlying shortage is not a recent phenomenon; it has persisted ever since special education became embedded in federal law following the passage of the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act in 1975.","Researchers and educators point out that a common misconception—that working with difficult children drives teachers away—fails to explain the crisis. Academic studies, including research by former special educator and Boston University professor Liz Bettini, indicate that teachers are largely motivated by student progress and community building. Instead, the heavy weight of the profession stems from severe isolation from other school staff, crushing volumes of mandatory paperwork, and the immense responsibility of teaching across multiple academic subjects and grade levels. Furthermore, studies reveal that special educators spend merely a third of their working day on actual instruction. The remaining time is consumed by tracking student goals, coordinating specialized therapies, training support staff, and drafting individualized education programs, known as IEPs. Kathleen Tejeda, a veteran educator in Riverside County, California, notes that advocacy is just as central to her profession as teaching, pointing to a blind freshman student who eventually made the cheerleading squad and choir.","These IEPs are legally mandated written documents that serve as essential roadmaps for each student with disabilities, outlining unique needs and annual objectives while ensuring instruction occurs in the least restrictive environment possible. However, managing IEPs requires extensive meetings with families, a deep understanding of legal frameworks, and hours taken away from classroom teaching. Behavioral challenges within classrooms can compound these pressures, sometimes forcing educators to manage emotional crises that disrupt entire learning environments, as third-year teacher Grace Fitzgerald experienced when an emotional crisis required evacuating her classroom. Additionally, institutional support remains uneven. While building relationships with students remains deeply rewarding, educators in 32 states receive no supplementary pay for taking on these high-demand positions, and only seventeen states currently offer additional financial compensation for hard-to-staff subjects.","The structural pipeline for new educators remains severely imbalanced. Policy research analyzing federal data demonstrates that approximately 46,000 special education teachers depart from public schools annually, whereas university preparation programs produce fewer than 30,000 new graduates each year to fill those vacancies. While some regions have experimented with targeted salary increases—such as Hawaii raising special education pay by $10,000 in 2020 and cutting its state vacancies by 35%—experts emphasize that closing the widening gap between supply and systemic demand will require comprehensive support, better compensation, and reduced administrative burdens."],"facts":[{"fact":"Special education teacher shortages exist in 45 out of 50 US states, according to the Learning Policy Institute.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"The number of special education students in public schools has climbed to more than 8.2 million, which is about 1 out of 6 students.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Research shows that special educators spend only about a third of their day on instruction, with the rest spent on tracking goals, coordinating services, training support staff, and writing IEPs.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Federal law has embedded special education since the passage of the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act in 1975.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"About 46,000 special education teachers leave public schools every year, while preparation programs train fewer than 